### TITLE ###
InGuardStripsIndexOfNull
### DESCRIPTION ###
The reported idiom. `index_of` returns int?, and an `in` guard proves
the lookup can't miss - but narrowing is keyed on symbols and
`xs.index_of(f)` isn't one, so the guard needs a membership fact to
reach it. One real script took this hint ten times off a single
flag-parsing loop.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str):
    if f in xs:
        n = xs.index_of(f) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  xs @ 2:13 -> str[]
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  xs @ 3:13 -> str[]
  index_of @ 3:16 -> dynamic
  f @ 3:25 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> void
  n (local): int
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
InGuardNarrowsPlainCallForm
### DESCRIPTION ###
Same fact, non-UFCS spelling. The container is args[0] here and the
receiver node for the UFCS form, so both paths have to resolve it.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str):
    if f in xs:
        n = index_of(xs, f) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  xs @ 2:13 -> str[]
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  index_of @ 3:13 -> dynamic
  xs @ 3:22 -> str[]
  f @ 3:26 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> void
  n (local): int
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
InGuardNarrowsStrSubject
### DESCRIPTION ###
`in` and index_of agree on strings too. Accepted hole, no way to see
it statically: `"" in s` is true for every s while `s.index_of("")`
is null, so an empty search target loses this hint.
### INPUT ###
fn g(s: str, sub: str):
    if sub in s:
        n = s.index_of(sub) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  sub @ 2:8 -> str
  s @ 2:15 -> str
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  s @ 3:13 -> str
  index_of @ 3:15 -> dynamic
  sub @ 3:24 -> str

# Symbol types
  g (fn): fn(str, str) -> void
  n (local): int
  s (param): str
  sub (param): str

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
AndChainCarriesMembershipToRightOperand
### DESCRIPTION ###
The `and` right operand runs only when the left held, so it inherits
the membership too. Safer than a branch body: no statement runs
between the operands, so nothing can invalidate the fact.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str):
    if f in xs and xs.index_of(f) > 0:
        n = 0
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  xs @ 2:13 -> str[]
  xs @ 2:20 -> str[]
  index_of @ 2:23 -> dynamic
  f @ 2:32 -> str
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> void
  n (local): int
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
NoGuardKeepsNull
### DESCRIPTION ###
Baseline: without a guard the null is real and the hint is correct.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str):
    n = xs.index_of(f) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  n @ 2:5 -> <no-type>
  xs @ 2:9 -> str[]
  index_of @ 2:12 -> dynamic
  f @ 2:21 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> void
  n (local): <error>
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30002 @ 2:9 - Invalid operand types: cannot do 'int? + int'

### TITLE ###
MembershipDoesNotEscapeTheBranch
### DESCRIPTION ###
Past the if, the guard says nothing - the fall-through path includes
the case where f was absent.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str):
    if f in xs:
        n = 0
    m = xs.index_of(f) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  xs @ 2:13 -> str[]
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  m @ 4:5 -> <no-type>
  xs @ 4:9 -> str[]
  index_of @ 4:12 -> dynamic
  f @ 4:21 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> void
  m (local): <error>
  n (local): int
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30002 @ 4:9 - Invalid operand types: cannot do 'int? + int'

### TITLE ###
MembershipRetiresWhenElseReturns
### DESCRIPTION ###
The else exits, so the guard really does hold on the fall-through -
and the fact still has to retire there. Its validity depends on a
reassignment scan that covered only the branch body; `xs = []` here
is outside anything we checked. joinFrames resets memberships to the
pre-if set for exactly this reason.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str):
    if f in xs:
        n = 0
    else:
        return
    xs = []
    m = xs.index_of(f) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  xs @ 2:13 -> str[]
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  xs @ 6:5 -> <no-type>
  m @ 7:5 -> <no-type>
  xs @ 7:9 -> list
  index_of @ 7:12 -> dynamic
  f @ 7:21 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> void
  m (local): <error>
  n (local): int
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30002 @ 7:9 - Invalid operand types: cannot do 'int? + int'

### TITLE ###
ElseBranchHasNoMembership
### DESCRIPTION ###
The else runs when the guard was false, which is the opposite of
proving membership.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str):
    if f in xs:
        n = 0
    else:
        m = xs.index_of(f) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  xs @ 2:13 -> str[]
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  m @ 5:9 -> <no-type>
  xs @ 5:13 -> str[]
  index_of @ 5:16 -> dynamic
  f @ 5:25 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> void
  m (local): <error>
  n (local): int
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30002 @ 5:13 - Invalid operand types: cannot do 'int? + int'

### TITLE ###
DifferentContainerDoesNotRefine
### DESCRIPTION ###
A guard on ys proves nothing about xs. The fact is keyed on the
symbol pair, so this can't collide.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], ys: str[], f: str):
    if f in ys:
        n = xs.index_of(f) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  ys @ 2:13 -> str[]
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  xs @ 3:13 -> str[]
  index_of @ 3:16 -> dynamic
  f @ 3:25 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str[], str) -> void
  n (local): <error>
  xs (param): str[]
  ys (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30002 @ 3:13 - Invalid operand types: cannot do 'int? + int'

### TITLE ###
ContainerReassignedInBodyDropsFact
### DESCRIPTION ###
Nothing rewrites a membership when the container is reassigned, the
way bindAssignTarget rewrites a narrowed type. So the fact is refused
up front when the body assigns either symbol.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str):
    if f in xs:
        xs = []
        n = xs.index_of(f) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  xs @ 2:13 -> str[]
  xs @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  n @ 4:9 -> <no-type>
  xs @ 4:13 -> list
  index_of @ 4:16 -> dynamic
  f @ 4:25 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> void
  n (local): <error>
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30002 @ 4:13 - Invalid operand types: cannot do 'int? + int'

### TITLE ###
NamedArgDisablesRefinement
### DESCRIPTION ###
`n` picks the Nth occurrence and `start` skips a prefix; membership
promises neither. Confirmed against the runtime - `f in xs` is true
while `xs.index_of(f, n=1)` returns null.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str):
    if f in xs:
        n = xs.index_of(f, n=1) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  xs @ 2:13 -> str[]
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  xs @ 3:13 -> str[]
  index_of @ 3:16 -> dynamic
  f @ 3:25 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> void
  n (local): <error>
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30002 @ 3:13 - Invalid operand types: cannot do 'int? + int'

### TITLE ###
MembershipDoesNotEscapeIntoALambda
### DESCRIPTION ###
Narrowings survive capture but memberships don't: the lambda can run
after an `xs.pop()`, or escape the guarded region entirely, and the
closure rule keys off reassignment so it sees neither.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str) -> fn() -> int:
    if f in xs:
        return fn() xs.index_of(f) + 1
    return fn() 0
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  xs @ 2:13 -> str[]
  xs @ 3:21 -> str[]
  index_of @ 3:24 -> dynamic
  f @ 3:33 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> fn() -> int
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30002 @ 3:21 - Invalid operand types: cannot do 'int? + int'

### TITLE ###
NotInGuardDoesNotRefine
### DESCRIPTION ###
`not in` holding is proof of absence. The guard-clause form of it
(`if f not in xs: return`) would prove presence on the fall-through,
but that fact has no region boundary to retire at - deliberately out
of scope.
### INPUT ###
fn g(xs: str[], f: str):
    if f not in xs:
        n = xs.index_of(f) + 1
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  f @ 2:8 -> str
  xs @ 2:17 -> str[]
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  xs @ 3:13 -> str[]
  index_of @ 3:16 -> dynamic
  f @ 3:25 -> str

# Symbol types
  f (param): str
  g (fn): fn(str[], str) -> void
  n (local): <error>
  xs (param): str[]

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30002 @ 3:13 - Invalid operand types: cannot do 'int? + int'
